<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elliott Cresson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1854">1854</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elliott Cresson was a Quaker merchant and philanthropist from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> and a leading advocate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, which settled free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-americans">African Americans</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. He toured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the early 1830s to raise funds for the cause. Port Cresson, the settlement that became Bassa Cove in Liberia, was named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elliott-cresson.jpg" alt="Elliott Cresson" width="320" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Cornelius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elliott_Cresson_photo_Restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Elliott Cresson was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on 2 March 1796, the first child of John Elliott Cresson and Mary Warder Cresson and the seventh generation of his family born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. After his father's death in 1814 he lived, unmarried, with his widowed mother at 730 Sansom Street for the rest of his life. In 1818 his uncle Caleb Cresson, Jr. gave him control of the prosperous mercantile business he had built, but in 1824 Cresson left it to give his time and money to philanthropy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cresson believed that freed slaves, surrounded by wealthier white people, could not easily rise, and that life in a mainly black society would change them for the better. He became the most active member of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>'s Young Men's Colonization Society, a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>, and from 1830 criticised the national body's debts and lack of accountability. After visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1832–1833 he went to Liberia in early 1833 to help found Port Cresson, today Buchanan, meant to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-john-river">Saint John River</a> and stop a trade of some 1200 slaves a month. In 1835 Bassa attackers incited by Spanish slave traders destroyed it, killing 20 of its 126 colonists; a month later a new colony was established at Bassa Cove.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a> turned against colonisation, Cresson wrote to him twice, and he was partly blamed when some Southern auxiliaries left the society. He toured the South in the late 1830s, where emancipation was held to depend on removing the freed slaves from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>A life member of the Franklin Institute from 1824, Cresson gave $1,000 in 1848 for the Elliott Cresson Medal, awarded from 1875 until 1998 to 268 people or groups. In 1853 he became the first president of the Philadelphia School of Design for Women, now Moore College of Art and Design. He died of gangrene in Philadelphia on 20 February 1854 and was buried at The Woodlands Cemetery, leaving the city a fund for planting shade trees; the town of Cresson, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Elliott Cresson” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the early 1830s the Young Men's Colonization Society of Pennsylvania, together with the New York colonisation society, founded a settlement at the mouth of the St John river, first called Port Cresson after the Quaker colonisation advocate <b>Elliott Cresson</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bassa-cove">Bassa Cove</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=39.95,-75.16&amp;year=1833" aria-label="Philadelphia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Elliott Cresson"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5962 4090h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5962 4090h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=39.95,-75.16&amp;year=1833">What was here in 1833? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
